.he "cp (1)                       8/12/26                       cp (1)"
.fo "                               -#-"
.in 5
.rm 65
.bd
NAME
.in +5
.br
.bd
cp
- copy files
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
SYNTAX
.in +5
.br
.bd 2
cp [ -i ] f1 f2
cp [ -ir ] f1 ... fn directory
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
DESCRIPTION
.in +5
.br
In the first form cp copies f1 onto f2.  In the second it copies each
file into the named directory, keeping its name.
.sp
Cp refuses to copy a file onto itself.  It compares the device and the
i-number to decide, so it catches the case reached by two different
paths as well as the obvious one.
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
OPTIONS
.in +5
.br
.in +10
.ti -10
-i	Ask before overwriting a file that exists.

.ti -10
-r	Copy a directory, and everything under it, recursively.

.in -10
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
SEE ALSO
.in +5
.br
ls (1), rm (1), mkdir (1)
